    <string name="info_privacy_body">TgWsProxy runs locally on the device and does not send your settings to an app-owned server. Diagnostic reports may include domains, routes and log fragments. Enable domain masking before publishing reports.</string>
    <string name="section_help">Help</string>
    <string name="tips_section_quick_start">Quick start</string>
    <string name="tips_section_telegram">Telegram SOCKS5</string>
    <string name="tips_section_modes">Connection modes</string>
    <string name="tips_section_worker">Cloudflare Worker</string>
    <string name="tips_section_cf">Cloudflare Proxy</string>
    <string name="tips_section_notification">Android notification</string>
    <string name="tips_quick_start">1. Set proxy port (usually 1081).\n2. Start TgWsProxy.\n3. In Telegram add SOCKS5: 127.0.0.1:1081 without login/password.\n4. Tap Apply in Telegram if available.\n5. If Telegram does not connect, open diagnostics.</string>
    <string name="tips_telegram">TgWsProxy listens on local SOCKS5. Telegram must connect to 127.0.0.1 and your chosen port. Avoid port conflicts with ByeDPI or other local proxies.</string>
    <string name="tips_modes">Auto is recommended. Worker first tries Worker then fallbacks. CF first tries CF proxy first. Worker only / CF only are strict. Direct only is for diagnostics.</string>
    <string name="tips_worker">Paste only the Worker hostname, for example example.username.workers.dev. Do not paste a full https:// URL with path.</string>
    <string name="tips_cf">CF proxy uses kws*.domain WebSocket hosts. Built-in and upstream domains are for testing; use your own domain for regular use.</string>
    <string name="tips_notification">Android requires a foreground notification while the proxy runs. You can make it compact or minimal, and control it from notification actions.</string>
